results.detailed_description:
Each finger bolts into a dark carrier through two of its own honeycomb holes, opened up to 3.2 mm for M3. The right carrier rides on your thumb and the left on your index finger, each held by a strap through two 4 × 22 mm slots. Those slots also take zip ties. The carrier holds a captive M4 lock nut, so the finger root isn't drilled. A 6 mm pivot plate sits above both carriers and holds the two M4 pivots at Gen 14's 27.137 mm spacing, so the closed tips still meet exactly as before. The hand frame bolts on top of the plate and runs back over the back of your hand to two wrist-strap slots. The camera cradle bolts onto the frame and holds an X4 or X5 upright on its own 1/4"-20 thread, lenses facing forward and back like the side and robot photos. Everything sits above the hand. A check confirms the thumb-index web stays empty at every angle from 0 to 30°.

The finger shape is Gen 14's. Gen 14's contact checks pass unchanged. I made four small changes for printing:
- the point is 2.0 mm thick instead of 1.6;
- every wall is at least 0.8 mm, two perimeters (the slot nearest the beak was 0.66 mm from the edge);
- the two bolt holes are opened up for M3;
- the dark tip pad prints merged with the finger. There's a two-material version if you want the pad in TPU.

The fingers print flat but need supports under the beak, because it thins on both faces. A flat-bottomed beak would have avoided that, but it changes the shape, so I left it.

Seven parts to print, about 150 cm³, all on a 220 mm bed. BOM.md has the hardware and the assembly steps. The hand itself is my guess: 22.5 mm fingers, a 60 × 44 mm wrist. The carrier's finger position, rail height and wrist-slot spacing are what to adjust after a first fit. One more: the camera stands 124 mm tall, like the photos, so it's top-heavy. The STLs are in exports/print/, which the repo's .gitignore keeps out of git. Rerun export_models.py to rebuild them.
